arXiv:2510.05496v1 [cs.IT] 07 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge
for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tadashi Wadayama
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Nagoya Institute of Technology
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wadayama@nitech.ac.jp</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a numerical method to evaluate mutual information (MI) in nonlinear Gaussian noise channels by using denoisingscore matching learning
for estimating the score function of channel output.
Via de Bruijn’s identity and the I–MMSE relation,
Fisher information estimated from the learned score yields
accurate estimates of MI through an integral representation of MI
for a variety of priors and channel nonlinearities
(e.g., elementwise nonlinearity).
In this work, we propose a comprehensive theoretical foundation for the Score-to-Fisher bridge methodology, along with practical guidelines for its implementation. We also conduct extensive validation experiments,
comparing our approach with closed-form solutions
and a kernel density estimation baseline. The results of our numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed method is both practical and efficient for mutual information estimation in nonlinear Gaussian noise channels.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider additive Gaussian noise channels with a deterministic nonlinear front-end,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and study how to estimate the mutual information <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently and accurately.
The nonlinear front-end can be represented by a deterministic function <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such models arise widely due to nonlinear front-end saturation/clipping, DAC/ADC nonlinearity,
photonic modulators, optical fibers, or learned encoders implemented with neural networks.
The value of the mutual information <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is important for evaluating the ultimate performance of coded communication systems and it provides useful information for designing the system such as coding, preprocessing, or decoding/detection algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is nonlinear, evaluating <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in general, challenging in practice because accurate
posterior means <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math> and normalized likelihoods are inaccessible or computationally intractable.
For linear Gaussian channels, the de Bruijn identity and the I–MMSE relation yield</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t})-\frac{n}{2t}=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mmse</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t})-\frac{n}{2t}=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">so that the mutual information <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by integrating either the Fisher information <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> or the MMSE.
Our key observation is that the deterministic nonlinearity can be absorbed into a transformed input
<math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t}),\quad Y_{t}=W+Z_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t}),\quad Y_{t}=W+Z_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">holds for <em id="S1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> deterministic function <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, for general <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> we can still rely on AWGN identities:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>mmse</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">and consequently obtain <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by integrating <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>mmse</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper,
we propose a score-based estimator of mutual information tailored to <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The method learns the score of channel output, or the marginal <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">using <span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">denoising score matching (DSM)</span>
and then obtain
an estimate of the Fisher information
<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{J}(Y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{J}(Y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math>
From this, we obtain an estimate of the mutual information via an integral representation of MI:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{I}(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\Big(\frac{n}{t}-\widehat{J}(Y_{t})\Big)\,dt." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{I}(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\Big(\frac{n}{t}-\widehat{J}(Y_{t})\Big)\,dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the approach <em id="S1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">never</em> requires access to the posterior <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X|Y}(\bm{x}|\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X|Y}(\bm{x}|\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.
All we need are forward samples <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\sim p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\sim p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
evaluations of <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}=f(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}=f(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and Gaussian noise addition,
which enables a <em id="S1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-loop</em> estimator of MMSE and the Fisher information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score-only MI estimation for nonlinear front-ends.</span>
We formalize a pipeline— denoising score matching of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Fisher estimation
<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> MMSE/MI integration—that applies to <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arbitrary</em> deterministic <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>
as soon as <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be sampled.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unified theoretical grounding.</span>
Leveraging de Bruijn and I–MMSE, we derive
<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>mmse</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}_{W}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and connect <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
extending linear-Gaussian identities to general nonlinear front-ends without ambiguity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical recipe.</span>
We detail implementation choices that make the estimator stable and reproducible:
logarithmic grids in <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, tail corrections for the MI integral, and a diagnostic “double
estimate” that compares a Tweedie-based direct MMSE with the Fisher-based MMSE.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validation and use-cases.</span>
We verify the estimator on Gaussian inputs where closed forms are available, and report
experiments on composite nonlinear channels.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In linear Gaussian channels, I–MMSE and multivariate de Bruijn provide differential and integral
representations of information quantities have been extensively studied.
For nonlinear <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, however, <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior-free</em>
and <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalization-free</em> MI estimators remain limited. Building on score matching and
denoising-based score learning, our approach attaches directly to the marginal <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
circumventing posterior computations and partition functions.
The estimate of mutual information can be used many applications such as
design of coding systems, optimization of preprocessing, and design of detection and decoding
algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless otherwise stated, all logarithms are natural (nats). Bold letters denote vectors in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a random vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with density <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(U)</annotation></semantics></math> for its differential entropy, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\cdot;\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\cdot;\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for mutual information, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Cov}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Cov}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> for its covariance matrix.
The (scalar) Fisher information is defined via the score function
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{U}(\bm{u})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{u}}\log p_{U}(\bm{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{U}(\bm{u})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{u}}\log p_{U}(\bm{u})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(U)\equiv\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|\nabla_{\bm{u}}\log p_{U}(\bm{u})\|^{2}\right]\quad\text{with }\bm{u}\sim p_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(U)\equiv\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|\nabla_{\bm{u}}\log p_{U}(\bm{u})\|^{2}\right]\quad\text{with }\bm{u}\sim p_{U}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Related works</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For channels with memory, simulation-based methods have been developed
to evaluate information rates without closed-form expressions.
Arnold <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proposed a simulation-based framework
that computes upper and lower bounds on the information rate by combining
auxiliary finite-state channel models with forward–backward recursions
and importance sampling; the method is broadly applicable to ISI and
finite-state channels where exact evaluation is infeasible.
Building on this line, Sadeghi <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>
optimized these bounds over the choice of auxiliary models, yielding
systematically improved estimates and a practical tool for design and
benchmarking of channels with memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another line of work estimates <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y)</annotation></semantics></math> directly from its definition via
simulation-based plug-in estimators: one approximates <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>)
using Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) or mixtures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> or employs <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbors
(<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN) estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. In the additive Gaussian setting,
recent theory shows that plug-in estimation of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X+Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X+Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact
optimal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AWGN channel and score function</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a channel input random vector
with known prior probability density function (PDF) <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and consider the AWGN channel family:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=X+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad t&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=X+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad t&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> represents the noise variance.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the marginal density of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The score function of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\equiv\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Throughout, we assume that sampling from <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is computationally feasible
by forward sampling or Langevin sampling using the score <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\bm{x}}\log p_{X}(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\bm{x}}\log p_{X}(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math>
(e.g., Rossky <span id="S2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Welling and Teh <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Fisher information of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(Y_{t})\equiv\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\bigl[\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\|^{2}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(Y_{t})\equiv\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\bigl[\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\|^{2}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Under standard regularity conditions,
the following Tweedie identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> holds for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}=\bm{y}]=\bm{y}+ts_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}=\bm{y}]=\bm{y}+ts_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I–MMSE relation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the SNR-parameterized AWGN model</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{\gamma}=\sqrt{\gamma}X+N,\quad N\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad N\perp X," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{\gamma}=\sqrt{\gamma}X+N,\quad N\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad N\perp X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the I–MMSE relation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> states that the identity</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\gamma}I(X;Y_{\gamma})=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mmse</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\gamma}I(X;Y_{\gamma})=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">holds for any snr <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma)\equiv\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|X-\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{\gamma}]\|^{2}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(\gamma)\equiv\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|X-\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{\gamma}]\|^{2}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">With the change of variables <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1/t</annotation></semantics></math> (and noting <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{\gamma})=I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{\gamma})=I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>),
an equivalent form for our variance parameterization <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\,I(X;Y_{t})=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mmse</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\,I(X;Y_{t})=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)\equiv\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|X-\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}]\|^{2}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)\equiv\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|X-\mathbb{E}[X|Y_{t}]\|^{2}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">de Bruijn identity</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the differential entropy of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the (multivariate) de Bruijn identity gives</p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}h(Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}h(Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The de Bruijn identity was discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">II-F</span> </span><span id="S2.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denoising score matching (DSM) method</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> provides a practical way to learn the score function
of a Gaussian-smoothed distribution without computing any normalization constants or partition functions.
Let <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be a target density on <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and define
a Gaussian-smoothed sample:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{v}=\bm{w}+\sigma\bm{\epsilon},\quad\bm{w}\sim p,\quad\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{n})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{v}=\bm{w}+\sigma\bm{\epsilon},\quad\bm{w}\sim p,\quad\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Writing <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\sigma}\equiv p*\phi_{\sigma^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\sigma}\equiv p*\phi_{\sigma^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the convolution with a zero-mean Gaussian PDF <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\sigma^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\sigma^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> with
variance <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the DSM loss function for a parametric score model <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\!\to\!\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\!\to\!\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the vector of adjustable parameters) is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{w},\bm{\epsilon}}\Big[\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{v})+\epsilon/\sigma\,\big\|^{2}\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{w},\bm{\epsilon}}\Big[\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{v})+\epsilon/\sigma\,\big\|^{2}\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{v}\sim p_{\sigma}}\Big[\big\|\,s_{\theta}(\bm{v})-\nabla_{\bm{v}}\log p_{\sigma}(\bm{v})\,\big\|^{2}\Big]+\text{const}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext>const</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{v}\sim p_{\sigma}}\Big[\big\|\,s_{\theta}(\bm{v})-\nabla_{\bm{v}}\log p_{\sigma}(\bm{v})\,\big\|^{2}\Big]+\text{const},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where this equivalence was shown by Vincent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. This means that
minimizing the DSM loss function is equivalent to minimizing the expectation of the squared distance between the learned score function and the true score function,
which is sometimes called the <span id="S2.SS6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fisher divergence</span>.
This means that the unique minimizer of the DSM loss function satisfies
<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y})\simeq\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{\sigma}(\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y})\simeq\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{\sigma}(\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> in high probability.
Equivalently, since <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}-\bm{w}=\sigma\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}-\bm{w}=\sigma\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, one may implement the loss function as <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{v}-\bm{w})/\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{v}-\bm{w})/\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{w},\bm{\epsilon}}\big[\|s_{\theta}(\bm{v})+(\bm{v}-\bm{w})/\sigma^{2}\,\|^{2}\big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{w},\bm{\epsilon}}\big[\|s_{\theta}(\bm{v})+(\bm{v}-\bm{w})/\sigma^{2}\,\|^{2}\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which is an equivalent form of the DSM loss function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we use a neural network to parameterize the score model <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{v})</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a score model is also called a score network. A mini-batch training with stochastic gradient descent or other variants such as Adam is typically used to minimize the DSM loss function.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Estimating Mutual Information in AWGN Channels</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a method to estimate
the MMSE and MI for AWGN channels described by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=X+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad t&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=X+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),\quad t&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The results obtained in this section will be
extended to general nonlinear channels in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Nonlinear channel ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMSE–Fisher relation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any Gaussian channel, the following proposition holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">holds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">A sketch of proof is the following.
Using <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=h(Y_{t})-h(Z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=h(Y_{t})-h(Z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(Z_{t})=(n/2)\log(2\pi e\,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(Z_{t})=(n/2)\log(2\pi e\,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t})-\frac{n}{2t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}J(Y_{t})-\frac{n}{2t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">by de Bruijn identity. Equating this with the I–MMSE form</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mmse</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}I(X;Y_{t})=-\frac{1}{2t^{2}}\mathrm{mmse}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">gives <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Reeves <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> investigated this identity in the
context of linear Gaussian channels, highlighting its implications for
non-Gaussian input distributions. Their work extends
the I–MMSE framework beyond the classical Gaussian setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Integral representations of mutual information</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the identity (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ III-A MMSE–Fisher relation ‣ III Estimating Mutual Information in AWGN Channels ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>)
and the fact that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\to\infty}I(X;Y_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\to\infty}I(X;Y_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
(since <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> become independent in the limit),
integrating over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\Big(\frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\Big)\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\Big(\frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\Big)\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\,dt." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\,dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The integrand decays as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, since</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\ \leq\ \frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\;=\;\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\ \leq\ \frac{\operatorname{tr}\operatorname{Cov}(X)}{t^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\ \leq\ \frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\;=\;\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\ \leq\ \frac{\operatorname{tr}\operatorname{Cov}(X)}{t^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The integral representation directly follows by combining the
de Bruijn identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> with the
I–MMSE relation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Estimation of Fisher Information</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using DSM method, we can estimate the score function of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=t</annotation></semantics></math>,
the learned score model coincides with the score of
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot)\approx\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot)\approx\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> can be implemented with a neural network with parameter vector <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This enables estimation of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}\|s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}\|s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from samples of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\sim p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\sim p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and Gaussian
noises.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, for a fixed noise level <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the
DSM loss function can be written as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{t}(\bm{\theta})\;\equiv\;\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon})+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\,\right\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{t}(\bm{\theta})\;\equiv\;\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon})+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\,\right\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})-s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\,\right\|^{2}\right]+\text{const}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext>const</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})-s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\,\right\|^{2}\right]+\text{const},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the constant term does not depend on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, with a
sufficiently rich function class for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(e.g., a universal neural network) and sufficiently many samples, any sequence of empirical minimizers
achieves
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})-s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\,\right\|^{2}\right]\rightarrow 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{Y_{t}}\!\left[\left\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})-s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\,\right\|^{2}\right]\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math>
i.e., the learned score converges to the true score
in the Fisher divergence sense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, with i.i.d. draws
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}=\bm{x}_{i}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}=\bm{x}_{i}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}(i=1,2,...,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}(i=1,2,...,N)</annotation></semantics></math>,
the Monte Carlo estimator:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{J}_{t}\triangleq\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}_{i})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{J}_{t}\triangleq\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y}_{i})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{J}_{t}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{J}_{t}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (law of large numbers),
and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N}\,(\widehat{J}_{t}-J(Y_{t}))\Rightarrow\mathcal{N}\big(0,\operatorname{Var}(\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\|^{2})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N}\,(\widehat{J}_{t}-J(Y_{t}))\Rightarrow\mathcal{N}\big(0,\operatorname{Var}(\|s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})\|^{2})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> (central limit theorem),
so the estimation error decays at rate <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="O(N^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice we plug in the learned score <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>; under Fisher divergence
consistency of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the plug-in estimator
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/N)\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/N)\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
is expected to be also consistent for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian input case for validation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we provide closed-form expressions for the Gaussian-input case
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\,\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\,\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in the AWGN channel family <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\,\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\,\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is well known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> that <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=(n/2)\log(1+P/t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=(n/2)\log(1+P/t)</annotation></semantics></math>;
see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> for the relation to MMSE.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed forms for Gaussian inputs)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\,\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\,\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\,\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\,\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>,
independent. Then, for all <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following closed forms:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\log\!\Big(1+\frac{P}{t}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}I(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\log\!\Big(1+\frac{P}{t}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}h(X\mid Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\log\!\Big(2\pi e\,\frac{Pt}{P+t}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}h(X\mid Y_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\log\!\Big(2\pi e\,\frac{Pt}{P+t}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)=n\,\frac{Pt}{P+t},\quad J(Y_{t})=\frac{n}{P+t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)=n\,\frac{Pt}{P+t},\quad J(Y_{t})=\frac{n}{P+t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch of proof: .</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the Gaussian model <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is known that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=(n/2)\log(1+P/t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=(n/2)\log(1+P/t)</annotation></semantics></math>
holds.
Moreover <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},(P+t)\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},(P+t)\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=-(P+t)^{-1}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=\nabla_{\bm{y}}\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=-(P+t)^{-1}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">and
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}\|s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}=n/(P+t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}\|s_{Y_{t}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}=n/(P+t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The remaining identities follow by substituting <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)=nt-t^{2}J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and by
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X\mid Y_{t})=h(Y_{t})-I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X\mid Y_{t})=h(Y_{t})-I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(Y_{t})=(n/2)\log\big((2\pi e)(P+t)\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(Y_{t})=(n/2)\log\big((2\pi e)(P+t)\big).</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Linear Gaussian channel case</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a linear Gaussian channel model:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a square, invertible matrix and
<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This setting preserves the Gaussian structure while introducing
nontrivial correlation through the linear mixing due to <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed forms for linear Gaussian channels)<span id="Thmproposition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> nonsingular, and
<math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, independent. Then for all <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Bigl(I_{n}+\tfrac{P}{t}AA^{\top}\Bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\Bigl(I_{n}+\tfrac{P}{t}AA^{\top}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{mmse}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{tr}\!\left(\Bigl(P^{-1}I_{n}+\tfrac{1}{t}A^{\top}A\Bigr)^{-1}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.670em;" width="0.670em"><mi>tr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{tr}\!\left(\Bigl(P^{-1}I_{n}+\tfrac{1}{t}A^{\top}A\Bigr)^{-1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{tr}\!\Bigl((PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})^{-1}\Bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.670em;" width="0.670em"><mi>tr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{tr}\!\Bigl((PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})^{-1}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch of proof:</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AX</annotation></semantics></math> is Gaussian with covariance <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PAA^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PAA^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The mutual information (<a href="#S3.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Proposition 3 (Closed forms for linear Gaussian channels). ‣ III-E Linear Gaussian channel case ‣ III Estimating Mutual Information in AWGN Channels ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) follows from the
determinant formula for Gaussian differential entropy
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
The Fisher information (<a href="#S3.E36" title="Equation 36 ‣ Proposition 3 (Closed forms for linear Gaussian channels). ‣ III-E Linear Gaussian channel case ‣ III Estimating Mutual Information in AWGN Channels ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) is obtained directly from
the Gaussian score function
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=-(PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})^{-1}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})=-(PAA^{\top}+tI_{n})^{-1}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The MMSE formula (<a href="#S3.E35" title="Equation 35 ‣ Proposition 3 (Closed forms for linear Gaussian channels). ‣ III-E Linear Gaussian channel case ‣ III Estimating Mutual Information in AWGN Channels ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) comes from
the linear MMSE estimator of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the identity between <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the Fisher information,
we can evaluate <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> by numerical integration.
In this section, we present details of the method to estimate the MMSE and MI
for the AWGN channel family.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Score-based Estimation Method for MI and MMSE</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed estimation algorithm is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Score-based Estimation Method for MI and MMSE ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The core of the algorithm is the DSM learning for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the estimation of the Fisher information from the score model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Score-to-Fisher bridge: Estimation Algorithm for MI and MMSE</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Prepare a logarithmically spaced grid over <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (details of the log grid are described in Subsection <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Log-domain trapezoid integration ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Sample generation: Generate a random mini-batch <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of
multiple pairs of <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> for mini-batch training with
forward sampling of <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="alg1.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\sim p_{X},\ \bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I}_{n})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\sim p_{X},\ \bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I}_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             Denoising score matching (DSM) learning for <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>: train the score model parameters by minimizing the empirical DSM loss:

<table id="S4.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{(\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon})\in\mathcal{B}}\big\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon})\;+\;\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\,\big\|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{(\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon})\in\mathcal{B}}\big\|\,s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon})\;+\;\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\,\big\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
Any stochastic optimizer,
such as Adam, can be applied to minimized the empirical loss function.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             Estimating the Fisher information: for each <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, generate <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{1},\ldots,\bm{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1},\ldots,\bm{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., by forward sampling <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}=\bm{x}_{i}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}=\bm{x}_{i}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and compute the estimated Fisher information:

<table id="S4.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{J}_{t}\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i})\big\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{J}_{t}\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i})\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
as an approximation of <math id="alg1.l4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             Estimating the MMSE:

<table id="S4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}(t)=nt-t^{2}\widehat{J}_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>mmse</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}(t)=nt-t^{2}\widehat{J}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             Estimating the MI via the integral representation:

<table id="S4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{I(X;Y_{T})}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\Big(\frac{n}{t}-\widehat{J}_{t}\Big)\,dt," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{I(X;Y_{T})}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\Big(\frac{n}{t}-\widehat{J}_{t}\Big)\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
evaluated numerically.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the above algorithm, we consider two alternatives for the DSM learning:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scheme A (per-<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> training).
For each fixed <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, minimize</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\bm{\epsilon})+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\big\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\big\|s_{\theta}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\bm{\epsilon})+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\big\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">to obtain the learned score model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scheme B (noise-conditional model).
Learn <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(\bm{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(\bm{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing</p>
<table id="S4.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{T}}\,\lambda(t)\ \mathbb{E}_{\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon}}\,\big\|s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon},t)+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\big\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{T}}\,\lambda(t)\ \mathbb{E}_{\bm{x},\bm{\epsilon}}\,\big\|s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{x}+\sqrt{t}\,\bm{\epsilon},t)+\bm{\epsilon}/\sqrt{t}\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a training distribution over <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., logarithmically spaced as described later)
and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight (e.g., <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)=t</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the case of Scheme B, the score network has two inputs: the channel output <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and the noise level <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
In generative modeling frameworks for image generation, Scheme B is often used to train a noise-conditional score model.
The training distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is logarithmically spaced as described in Subsection <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Log-domain trapezoid integration ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>.
The weight <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight function that is used to weight the loss function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Validating the Fisher-based estimate</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Score-based Estimation Method for MI and MMSE ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presented in the previous subsection
is an asymptotically consistent method to estimate the MMSE and MI,
the accuracy of the estimate is not clear in the case of finite samples.
One possible source of the error is the estimation error of the Fisher information due to inaccurate estimation of the score model.
Although we can expect the accurate score matching in the Fisher divergence sense in the case of infinite samples, the convergence rate depends on the prior distribution of the source. We also have to consider the numerical error of the numerical integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a method to validate the estimated MMSE and MI.
As a diagnostic purpose, we can compute
the direct MMSE estimate based on the Tweedie formula:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}_{\rm dir}(t)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\big\|\bm{x}_{i}-\{\bm{y}_{i}(t)+t\,s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i}(t))\}\big\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>mmse</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>dir</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}_{\rm dir}(t)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\big\|\bm{x}_{i}-\{\bm{y}_{i}(t)+t\,s_{\theta}(\bm{y}_{i}(t))\}\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and check agreement with the Fisher-based estimate:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}_{F}(t)\equiv nt-t^{2}\widehat{J}_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>mmse</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathrm{mmse}}_{F}(t)\equiv nt-t^{2}\widehat{J}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This consistency check is useful to validate the accuracy of the Fisher-based estimate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Log-domain trapezoid integration</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integrand in the integration representation of MI is
smooth and may have a long tail. The choice of the integration grid is
critical for achieving high numerical accuracy. In this subsection,
we present a geometric (log-spaced) grid for the integration.
For <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[t_{\min},t_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[t_{\min},t_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> points, take <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log t</annotation></semantics></math> to be equally spaced:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle t_{k}=t_{\min}\left(\frac{t_{\max}}{t_{\min}}\right)^{\frac{k}{M-1}},\qquad k=0,1,\ldots,M-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t_{k}=t_{\min}\left(\frac{t_{\max}}{t_{\min}}\right)^{\frac{k}{M-1}},\qquad k=0,1,\ldots,M-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Equivalently in standard deviation, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}\equiv\sqrt{t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}\equiv\sqrt{t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}=\sigma_{\min}(\sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min})^{k/(M-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}=\sigma_{\min}(\sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min})^{k/(M-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than applying the trapezoid rule directly on the non-uniform <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-grid,
we perform the change of variables <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log t</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain uniform spacing in the log domain.
Using the integral representation</p>
<table id="S4.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\left(\frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\right)\,dt," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{T}^{\infty}\!\left(\frac{n}{t}-J(Y_{t})\right)\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and substituting <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log t</annotation></semantics></math> (so <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dt=e^{u}du" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dt=e^{u}du</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=e^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=e^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain</p>
<table id="S4.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\log T}^{\infty}\!\Big(n-e^{u}\,J(Y_{e^{u}})\Big)\,du." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{T})=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\log T}^{\infty}\!\Big(n-e^{u}\,J(Y_{e^{u}})\Big)\,du.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-grid (<a href="#S4.E44" title="Equation 44 ‣ IV-C Log-domain trapezoid integration ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) corresponds to uniform spacing
in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log t</annotation></semantics></math> with step size <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}\equiv\log(t_{\max}/t_{\min})/(M-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}\equiv\log(t_{\max}/t_{\min})/(M-1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we apply the standard trapezoid rule (uniform spacing):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{I}(X;Y_{T})\approx\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=k_{T}}^{M-2}\frac{\ell(u_{k})+\ell(u_{k+1})}{2}\,\Delta_{u}\;+\;\text{tail}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mtext>tail</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{I}(X;Y_{T})\approx\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=k_{T}}^{M-2}\frac{\ell(u_{k})+\ell(u_{k+1})}{2}\,\Delta_{u}\;+\;\text{tail},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(u)\equiv n-e^{u}\,\widehat{J}(Y_{e^{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(u)\equiv n-e^{u}\,\widehat{J}(Y_{e^{u}})</annotation></semantics></math>
(equivalently <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(t)=n-t\,\widehat{J}(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(t)=n-t\,\widehat{J}(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>),
and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest index with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k_{T}}\geq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k_{T}}\geq T</annotation></semantics></math>.
This log-domain integration offers several benefits over direct <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-domain
trapezoid integration: (1) uniform grid spacing reduces discretization error,
(2) the transformation naturally handles a wide dynamic range in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(3) convexity bias is significantly reduced. In our experiments,
this approach improved MI estimation accuracy by over an order of magnitude.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The tail contribution above <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated using the asymptotic behavior.
For Gaussian inputs, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)\to\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)\to\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, giving</p>
<table id="S4.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{tail}\approx\frac{1}{2}\int_{t_{\max}}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\,dt\approx\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)}{t_{\max}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>tail</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{tail}\approx\frac{1}{2}\int_{t_{\max}}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{mmse}(t)}{t^{2}}\,dt\approx\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)}{t_{\max}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The trace <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{tr}\mathrm{Cov}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated from samples of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and used as a tail correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While the tail correction formula (<a href="#S4.E48" title="Equation 48 ‣ IV-C Log-domain trapezoid integration ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) is exact for Gaussian inputs,
its leading-order term <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{tr}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X)/t_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{tr}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X)/t_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>
remains valid for arbitrary inputs, since <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mmse}(t)\to\mathrm{tr}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>mmse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mmse}(t)\to\mathrm{tr}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, the same tail correction can be applied as a universal approximation,
with the understanding that higher-order terms may depend on the non-Gaussianity of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Nonlinear channel</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider our primal target channel:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=f(X)+Z_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic function. The mutual information equivalence
presented in the following proposition is a key observation which enables the score-based estimation to be applied to general nonlinear channels.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mutual information equivalence</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\equiv f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\equiv f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a measurable deterministic mapping, and consider <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=W+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=W+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},t\bm{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},t\bm{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the conditional law of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> only through <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
This means that <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to W\to Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to W\to Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a Markov chain and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\perp X\mid W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\perp X\mid W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can prove <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mutual information equivalence</span> in the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=W+Z_{t}(t&gt;0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=W+Z_{t}(t&gt;0)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},t\bm{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},t\bm{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,W)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a measurable deterministic function.
Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the chain rule of mutual information, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})+I(X;Y_{t}\mid W)-I(W;Y_{t}\mid X)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})+I(X;Y_{t}\mid W)-I(W;Y_{t}\mid X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t}\mid W)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t}\mid W)=0</annotation></semantics></math> because of the conditional independence <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\perp X\mid W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\perp X\mid W</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(W;Y_{t}\mid X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(W;Y_{t}\mid X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> holds because <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
An equivalent proof uses the data processing inequality twice: <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to W\to Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to W\to Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})\leq I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})\leq I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to X\to Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to X\to Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>) gives <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(W;Y_{t})\leq I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(W;Y_{t})\leq I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding equality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ V-A Mutual information equivalence ‣ V Nonlinear channel ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we have
the MI equivalence <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, which
implies that the score-based estimation method presented in the previous section can be applied to evaluate <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Namely, instead of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, from the samples of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can estimate the Fisher information of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the estimate of the Fisher information can be used
to evaluate <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The equivalence relation <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
enables to obtain the estimate of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from the estimate of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that no invertibility of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is required in
this estimation method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Score-based estimation method for nonlinear channels</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following discussin, we assume that the evalution of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is
computationally feasible. Namely, sampling from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible.
Some parts of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Score-based Estimation Method for MI and MMSE ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are
replaced by the following steps:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimate the Fisher information of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the samples of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> by the score-based estimation method in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Score-based Estimation Method for MI and MMSE ‣ IV Estimating MMSE and Mutual Information ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use the estimated Fisher information to evaluate the integral form of MI.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use the relationship <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=I(W;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the estimate of <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The estimation procedure is still a single-loop procedure, which does not require the access to the posterior <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X|Y_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X|Y_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Numerical experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian input case for validation</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the proposed score-based estimation method, we first consider the
Gaussian input case where exact closed-form expressions are available.
Specifically, we consider the Gaussian input case <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(0,PI_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(0,PI_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with the AWGN channel model <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X+\sqrt{t}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X+\sqrt{t}Z</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implemented the DSM method with the following configuration:
The score network is implemented as a fully connected neural network consisting of 3 hidden layers,
each containing 128 units with SiLU (Swish) activation functions. The input and output dimensions match
the problem dimension <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, where we test with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate scalability.
For the training configuration, we set the signal power to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> and construct a logarithmically
spaced grid of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=10</annotation></semantics></math> noise variance values ranging from <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[P/200,200P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>200,200</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[P/200,200P]</annotation></semantics></math>, which covers four
orders of magnitude. At each noise level <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we train a separate score network for 300 iterations
using mini-batches of 4096 samples. The optimization is performed using the Adam optimizer with a
learning rate of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gradient clipping is applied with a maximum norm of 1.0 to ensure
stable training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>2 </span>Validating the Fisher Information Estimate</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Fisher information estimation, we generate 100,000 Monte Carlo samples at each noise variance <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
to ensure statistical reliability. The ground truth values are computed using the analytical formula
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is available for Gaussian input distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ VI-A2 Validating the Fisher Information Estimate ‣ VI-A Gaussian input case for validation ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the estimated Fisher information <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{J}(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{J}(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> compared with
the analytical ground truth <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)</annotation></semantics></math> for dimensions <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The results
demonstrate excellent agreement between the DSM-based estimates and theoretical values across multiple
orders of magnitude in the noise variance <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/fisher_multi_n_fisher.svg" id="S6.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S6.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fisher information <math id="S6.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> versus noise variance <math id="S6.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for Gaussian input with <math id="S6.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
dimensions <math id="S6.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Solid lines with circular markers show ground truth values <math id="S6.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y_{t})=n/(P+t)</annotation></semantics></math>, while dashed lines with triangular markers show DSM estimates <math id="S6.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{J}(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{J}(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results confirm that:</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The DSM-based score estimation accurately captures the Fisher information across a wide range
of noise levels (<math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0.005,200]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.005,200</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0.005,200]</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The method scales well with dimension, maintaining low relative errors even for <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The median relative error remains below 1.3% for all tested dimensions, validating the
theoretical framework.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>3 </span>Validating the Fisher-based MMSE Estimate</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our Fisher-based MMSE estimation, we compare three methods using the same experimental
setup with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>: (1) true MMSE (analytical ground truth), (2) Tweedie-based direct estimation using</p>
<table id="S6.EGx46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\text{mmse}}_{\text{dir}}(t)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|\bm{x}_{i}-(\bm{y}_{i}+ts_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y}_{i}))\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mtext>mmse</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>dir</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\text{mmse}}_{\text{dir}}(t)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\|\bm{x}_{i}-(\bm{y}_{i}+ts_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y}_{i}))\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and (3) our Fisher-based method using</p>
<table id="S6.EGx47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\text{mmse}}_{\text{F}}(t)=nt-t^{2}\hat{J}(Y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mtext>mmse</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>F</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\text{mmse}}_{\text{F}}(t)=nt-t^{2}\hat{J}(Y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The noise variance range is set to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[P/200,50P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[P/200,50P]</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid numerical instabilities at extremely high noise levels. The results are shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ VI-A3 Validating the Fisher-based MMSE Estimate ‣ VI-A Gaussian input case for validation ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The two estimates are in excellent agreement with the true MMSE in the wide range of noise variance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/validation_comparison_mmse.svg" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S6.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of MMSE estimation methods for Gaussian input (<math id="S6.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math>). </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>4 </span>Mutual Information Estimation</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate the complete pipeline from
the Score-to-Fisher bridge for mutual information estimation using the integral
representation of MI.
The experiment uses the same configuration as previous tests with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, implementing
log-domain trapezoid integration with tail correction.
Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-A4 Mutual Information Estimation ‣ VI-A Gaussian input case for validation ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the estimated mutual information (per symbol)
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}(X;Y_{t})/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}(X;Y_{t})/n</annotation></semantics></math> compared with
the analytical ground truth <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})/n=(1/2)\log(1+P/t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})/n=(1/2)\log(1+P/t)</annotation></semantics></math> for Gaussian input. The DSM-based
estimates demonstrate excellent agreement with the theoretical values across the entire noise variance
range.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/MI_vs_t_P1p0.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/175;" width="229" height="175"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mutual information (per symbol) <math id="S6.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})/n</annotation></semantics></math> versus noise variance <math id="S6.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for Gaussian input. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key contribution of this experiment is the log-domain trapezoid integration method. Compared to standard
trapezoid integration on the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-grid, the log-domain approach improved estimation accuracy by over an
order of magnitude, reducing median relative errors from approximately 26% to 0.4%. This substantial
improvement arises from: (1) uniform grid spacing in the log domain, (2) reduced convexity bias, and
(3) better numerical handling of the wide dynamic range in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The tail correction, based on the asymptotic behavior <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{mmse}(t)\sim\text{tr Cov}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>mmse</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>tr Cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{mmse}(t)\sim\text{tr Cov}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for large
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, proves essential for maintaining accuracy across the full integration range. Without tail
correction, the 90th percentile error degrades significantly to over 40%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These validation results on Gaussian inputs, where analytical solutions exist, provide strong
evidence for the reliability of the proposed Score-to-Fisher bridge method and related theoretical framework presented in the previous sections.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>5 </span>Noise-conditional model</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate a noise-conditional score model <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bm{\theta}}(\bm{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> (Scheme B)
that takes both
observation <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and noise variance <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs, eliminating the need for separate
per-<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> training. The model uses Gaussian Fourier projection to embed scalar <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
values, which are injected into each hidden layer. Training samples <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> from a
log-uniform distribution over <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Pt_{\min},Pt_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Pt_{\min},Pt_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> with loss
weighting <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)=t</annotation></semantics></math> for 20,000 steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For Gaussian input with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, this approach achieves 6.61% median
relative error across 12 evaluation points spanning <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0.005,50]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.005</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0.005,50]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ VI-A5 Noise-conditional model ‣ VI-A Gaussian input case for validation ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrates reasonable agreement with analytical
values across four orders of magnitude in noise variance. We can observe the tendency that the conditional DSM estimate slightly underestimates the MI at low noise variance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/MI_vs_t_Conditional_P1p0.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/175;" width="229" height="175"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Noise-conditional model for mutual information estimation results for Gaussian input (<math id="S6.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Scheme B was used.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This unified framework requires only one neural network instead of 12 separate models,
significantly reducing computational cost while eliminating the extreme outliers
observed in per-<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> training (Scheme A) and providing smooth interpolation across the entire noise
range.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Discrete input case</span></h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>1 </span>Mutual Information Estimation</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the approach for discrete input distributions, we consider Binary Phase
Shift Keying (BPSK) modulation with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\{-\sqrt{P},+\sqrt{P}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\{-\sqrt{P},+\sqrt{P}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (equal
probability), <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and dimension <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike continuous distributions, BPSK
admits exact mutual information calculation via numerical integration of the Gaussian mixture distribution providing ground truth for validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We implement per-<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> training (Scheme A) over <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=12</annotation></semantics></math> logarithmically spaced points in
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0.005,50]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.005</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0.005,50]</annotation></semantics></math>, with 1000 training iterations per noise level and 200,000 Monte
Carlo samples for Fisher information estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ VI-B1 Mutual Information Estimation ‣ VI-B Discrete input case ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares DSM estimates with exact BPSK mutual information
and the Gaussian input baseline <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{Gauss}}(X;Y_{t})/n=\frac{1}{2}\log(1+P/t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Gauss</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{Gauss}}(X;Y_{t})/n=\frac{1}{2}\log(1+P/t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The DSM estimates achieve excellent agreement with exact values, with median relative
errors of 0.6% for low noise (<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;0.1</annotation></semantics></math>) and 2.8% for mid-range (<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1\leq t&lt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1\leq t&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/bpsk_mi_comparison.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/174;" width="229" height="174"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mutual information estimation for BPSK input (<math id="S6.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Dotted line: Gaussian input theoretical baseline;
solid circles: BPSK exact MI via numerical integration;
dashed squares: DSM estimates. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Linear Gaussian channel case</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the method beyond the standard AWGN case, we consider the linear Gaussian channel:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t},\qquad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random orthogonal matrix and
<math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},P\mathbf{I}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We train separate score networks (3 hidden layers, 128 units each) for <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=10</annotation></semantics></math> logarithmically spaced noise levels in <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[P/200,50P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[P/200,50P]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each network is trained for 300 iterations with 8192-sample mini-batches, and Fisher information is estimated using 100,000
Monte Carlo samples per noise level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ VI-C Linear Gaussian channel case ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares the DSM-based estimates
with the analytical solution (per symbol)
<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})/n=(1/2)\log\det(I_{n}+(P/t)AA^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})/n=(1/2)\log\det(I_{n}+(P/t)AA^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results demonstrate excellent agreement across 4 orders of magnitude in noise variance, confirming that the
Score-to-Fisher bridge method generalizes successfully to linear Gaussian channels with nontrivial correlation structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/MI_linear_vs_t.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/175;" width="229" height="175"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mutual information estimation for linear Gaussian channel <math id="S6.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=AX+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Circles: analytical ground truth; triangles: DSM estimates.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Nonlinear channel case</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method for nonlinear channels where closed-form solutions are
unavailable, we consider a composite channel with linear mixing followed by elementwise tanh nonlinearity:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t},\quad Z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},t\mathbf{I}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random orthogonal matrix and <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is applied elementwise.
This model represents practical scenarios where signals undergo linear transformations before nonlinear processing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span>1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same DSM configuration as in the linear case: <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=12</annotation></semantics></math> logarithmically spaced noise
levels in <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[P/200,50P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[P/200,50P]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The orthogonal matrix <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is generated via QR decomposition of a random Gaussian matrix.
For each <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we train score networks for 400 iterations using 8192-sample mini-batches.
Fisher information estimation uses 100,000 Monte Carlo samples per noise level.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS4.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span>2 </span>KDE-LOO Baseline</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since analytical solutions are unavailable for nonlinear channels, we implement a Kernel Density Estimation with
Leave-One-Out (KDE-LOO) baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>
that directly estimates mutual information from its definition.
For samples <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{w}_{j},\bm{y}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{w}_{j},\bm{y}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{j}=\bm{w}_{j}+\sqrt{t}\bm{\epsilon}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{j}=\bm{w}_{j}+\sqrt{t}\bm{\epsilon}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the KDE-LOO estimator is defined by</p>
<table id="S6.EGx50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{I}_{\text{KDE}}(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Bigg[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>KDE</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{I}_{\text{KDE}}(X;Y_{t})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Bigg[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{\|\bm{y}_{i}-\bm{w}_{i}\|^{2}}{2t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{\|\bm{y}_{i}-\bm{w}_{i}\|^{2}}{2t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E55.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\log\left(\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\neq i}e^{-\frac{\|\bm{y}_{i}-\bm{w}_{j}\|^{2}}{2t}}\right)\Bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\log\left(\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\neq i}e^{-\frac{\|\bm{y}_{i}-\bm{w}_{j}\|^{2}}{2t}}\right)\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This estimator is derived from the plug-in principle applied to <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}[\log p_{Y_{t}|X}(\bm{y}|\bm{x})]-\mathbb{E}[\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y_{t})=\mathbb{E}[\log p_{Y_{t}|X}(\bm{y}|\bm{x})]-\mathbb{E}[\log p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})]</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the marginal density <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{t}}(\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated using LOO kernel density
estimation to avoid overfitting.
We use <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=20,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=20,000</annotation></semantics></math> samples with full-sum computation (no <math id="S6.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbor approximation) to ensure high accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS4.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span>3 </span>Results and Discussion</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ VI-D3 Results and Discussion ‣ VI-D Nonlinear channel case ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents the comparison between DSM-based estimates and the KDE-LOO baseline for the
composite nonlinear channel.
The results demonstrate remarkable agreement between the two methods across the entire noise variance range, with both
curves nearly overlapping.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/MI_tanh_linear_DSM_vs_KDE_fullsum.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/172;" width="229" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 7</span>: </span><span id="S6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mutual information estimation for composite nonlinear channel <math id="S6.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Circles: KDE-LOO baseline; triangles: DSM estimates. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Key observations include:</p>
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Excellent agreement</span>: The DSM estimates closely match the KDE-LOO baseline across four orders of magnitude
in noise variance, with relative errors typically below 5%.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational efficiency</span>: While KDE-LOO requires <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> distance computations per noise level, DSM
achieves comparable accuracy with <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> score evaluations after one-time training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nonlinear validation</span>: This result provides strong evidence for the Score-to-Fisher bridge approach in
scenarios where analytical benchmarks are unavailable.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The success of this experiment confirms that the proposed method generalizes effectively to handle composite channels
combining linear mixing and nonlinear transformations, making it a practical tool for mutual information estimation
in realistic communication systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-E</span> </span><span id="S6.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Internal validation via Fisher information</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the theoretical foundations of our approach, we conduct an internal consistency check by estimating Fisher information through two independent routes within the proposed framework. For the same composite nonlinear channel <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=\tanh(AX)+Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compare:</p>
<ol id="S6.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score-based route</span>: <math id="S6.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\text{score}}(Y_{t})\equiv\mathbb{E}[\|s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\text{score}}(Y_{t})\equiv\mathbb{E}[\|s_{\bm{\theta}}(Y_{t})\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> from trained score networks</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMSE-based route</span>: <math id="S6.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\text{mmse}}(Y_{t})\equiv{(nt-\text{mmse}_{W}(t))}/{t^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>mmse</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>mmse</mtext><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\text{mmse}}(Y_{t})\equiv{(nt-\text{mmse}_{W}(t))}/{t^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> from trained denoisers</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Both routes should yield identical Fisher information if the theoretical framework is sound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ VI-E Internal validation via Fisher information ‣ VI Numerical experiments ‣ Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents the comparison between the two estimation routes across the noise variance range.
At the low noise variance, the MMSE-based route underestimates the Fisher information due to the difficulty of the learning of the optimal denoiser at low noise variance.
However, at the mid to high noise variance, the two routes show excellent agreement,
with both curves nearly overlapping throughout most of the range.
This internal consistency check provides strong evidence for the correctness of our theoretical approach and implementation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05496v1/J_compare_tanhA.svg" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/171;" width="229" height="171"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 8</span>: </span><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Internal validation via Fisher information estimation. Circles: score-based route; triangles: MMSE-based route. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This validation experiment is particularly valuable as it provides a self-contained verification of the method’s theoretical consistency, independent of external baselines or ground truth comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have presented a novel approach for mutual information estimation in nonlinear Gaussian noise channels via the Score-to-Fisher bridge. The method leverages DSM to estimate Fisher information, which is then converted to mutual information through the I-MMSE relation and log-domain integration with tail correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental validation across linear Gaussian channels, composite nonlinear channels, and internal Fisher consistency checks demonstrates excellent agreement with analytical solutions and baseline methods. The approach achieves computational efficiency with <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluations per noise level while maintaining high accuracy across wide dynamic ranges of the noise variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Score-to-Fisher bridge methodology provides a practical and theoretically sound framework for mutual information estimation in realistic nonlinear Gaussian noise
channel models where closed-form solutions are unavailable.
Accurate estimation of mutual information plays a crucial role in assessing the fundamental limits of coded communication systems, and also serves as a valuable guideline for system design choices such as coding schemes, preprocessing strategies, and detection or decoding algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

